//! lotus/voiced.rye -- ALES94: the voiced/unvoiced/silent classifier -- the first Lotus rung to FUSE two analysis readers into one verdict.
//! ALES93 gave the suite its first analysis reader -- a count of the signal's genuine crossings. ALES13 already carried the other classic feature -- the RMS
//! energy. This rung is the first to fuse two readers into one verdict: the oldest decision in speech processing, is this frame VOICED, UNVOICED, or SILENT? A
//! century of telephony, every speech codec, every voice-activity gate answers it the same way, from exactly these two features. ENERGY (RMS) separates sound
//! from silence -- below a floor there is nothing to classify. CROSSING RATE separates the two kinds of sound above that floor -- a VOICED sound (a vowel, a
//! hummed note, a periodic tone) carries most of its energy low and crosses FEW times; an UNVOICED sound (a fricative sss, a hiss, broadband noise) carries its
//! energy high and crosses MANY times.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: if rms < silence_floor -> Silent; else if crossings ≥ voice_split -> Unvoiced; else -> Voiced. The energy gate comes FIRST -- a signal
//! that crosses many times but sits below the floor is SILENT, not unvoiced (a quiet noise floor is silence, not a fricative). Reading the floor before the
//! crossing rate is the rule's one ordering invariant.
//!
//! The crux: this rung invents no new measurement. Its whole correctness is that its verdict equals a decision built BY HAND from ALES93's count_crossings and
//! ALES13's RMS over the SAME span -- the classifier is the FUSION RULE, not a third reader that could disagree with the two it names. So it can never drift from
//! the two readers it composes, exactly as ALES93 can never drift from the trigger it counts.
//!
//! The floor-zero convention: a silence_floor of 0 DISABLES the silence verdict (rms < 0 is never true), so an all-zero span (rms 0, zero crossings) classifies
//! VOICED (the low-crossing branch). A keeper who wants true silence caught sets the floor to at least 1. Stated plainly so the degenerate is deliberate.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES94.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260814/20260814-230352_fill-ales94-lotus-voiced-unvoiced-classifier.md
//! Stands on: lotus/zero_cross.rye (ALES93) -- the crossing count, one feature, read through the ALES92 band; lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) -- the RMS energy, the
//! other feature; lotus/schmitt.rye (ALES92) -- the band's precheck (so the band-and-span law and named errors are the trigger's own); lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2)
//! -- the Clip, sample_min/max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL, and READ-ONLY. A bounded in-process buffer of i16 PCM on one bench, siloed to lotus/, never mutated by this rung.
//! The verdict is a three-valued label over one span, NOT a phoneme, not a transcript, not a probability: a real voice-activity detector adds hysteresis over
//! time, adaptive floors, and a spectral tilt this rung does not -- those are later rungs. The "voiced/unvoiced" names follow the classic speech-processing
//! convention over exactly two features (energy and crossing rate); they claim nothing about a real speaker, language, or recording. No real sample rate, no
//! windowing over time, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/voiced.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const schmitt = @import("schmitt.rye");
const zero_cross = @import("zero_cross.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const voiced_version = "20260814.230352";

/// The classifier's faults are the two readers' OWN faults, reused by name so the classifier never invents an error the readers do not raise: ALES93's
/// ZeroCrossError (the band-and-span faults BadThreshold/BadRange from ALES92, and CountFull) and ALES13's MeterError (MeterFull) -- plus one of its own for an
/// illegal energy floor. CountFull and MeterFull cannot fire here (a single span is at most timeline.max_clip samples, far below either reader's bound), yet the
/// union stays honest about what the composed readers may raise rather than hiding a path the type system can see.
pub const ClassifyError = zero_cross.ZeroCrossError || meter.MeterError || error{
    /// The silence floor is above the peak magnitude ceiling -- no RMS could ever reach it, so every span would read silent, a meaningless setting. Refused
    /// before any feature is read. (A floor of 0 is legal -- it deliberately disables the silence verdict.)
    BadFloor,
};

/// The three-valued outcome of one classification over one span -- the oldest decision in speech processing. `silent` below the energy floor, `voiced` above it
/// with a low crossing rate (periodic sound), `unvoiced` above it with a high crossing rate (noise, fricatives).
pub const Verdict = enum {
    /// Below the energy floor -- nothing to classify.
    silent,
    /// Loud with a low crossing rate -- a periodic sound (vowel, note, tone).
    voiced,
    /// Loud with a high crossing rate -- a noisy sound (fricative, hiss, broadband).
    unvoiced,
};

/// Classify `count` samples starting at `start` as silent, voiced, or unvoiced -- reading the crossing count through the ALES92 band `[t_low, t_high]` and the
/// RMS energy over the SAME span, then fusing them by the one rule: below `silence_floor` is silent; at or above `voice_split` crossings is unvoiced; otherwise
/// voiced. The energy gate is read FIRST. READ-ONLY -- not one sample is written. Validates the band and span through ALES92's own precheck (BadThreshold on an
/// illegal band, BadRange on a span outside the samples) and the floor against the peak ceiling (BadFloor) before reading either feature; on refusal nothing is
/// read into a verdict and the clip is untouched.
pub fn classify(
    clip: *const timeline.Clip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    t_low: i32,
    t_high: i32,
    silence_floor: u32,
    voice_split: u32,
) ClassifyError!Verdict {
    // invariant: the band and span are the two readers' own law -- one precheck shared with ALES92/ALES93, so the classifier can never validate differently than
    // the counter it composes.
    try schmitt.precheck(clip.len, start, count, t_low, t_high);
    // invariant: a floor no RMS could ever reach would make every span silent -- refuse it rather than answer meaninglessly. A floor of 0 is legal (it disables
    // the silence verdict), so the ceiling is the only illegal floor.
    if (silence_floor > meter.sample_peak_max) return error.BadFloor;

    // Feature one -- the RMS energy over exactly this span (ALES13), read from a fresh meter fed the span's samples, never the whole clip.
    var m = meter.Meter{};
    const samples = clip.samples();
    try meter.feed(&m, samples[start .. start + count]);
    const rms: u32 = meter.rms(&m);

    // The energy gate FIRST: below the floor is silence, whatever the crossing rate -- a quiet noise floor is silence, not a fricative.
    if (rms < silence_floor) return .silent;

    // Feature two -- the genuine crossing count over the same span (ALES93), read through the same band. Only reached once the span is known loud enough to carry
    // a real sound.
    const crossings: u32 = try zero_cross.count_crossings(clip, start, count, t_low, t_high);

    // invariant: the reader that ran cannot have changed the clip's length -- both features are read-only.
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);

    // The crossing gate SECOND: a high rate is unvoiced (noise), a low rate voiced (periodic).
    if (crossings >= voice_split) return .unvoiced;
    return .voiced;
}

// --- selftest: loud-and-periodic -> voiced; loud-and-noisy -> unvoiced; quiet -> silent; the floor dominates the crossing rate; the verdict is exactly the two
//     readers fused (the crux); the clip is byte-for-byte unchanged (read-only); the floor-zero convention; an illegal floor, band, and span each refuse by
//     name; the span discipline holds ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

/// The hand-built fusion the classifier is checked against in the crux -- the SAME rule read from the two readers directly.
fn by_hand(clip: *const timeline.Clip, start: u32, count: u32, t_low: i32, t_high: i32, floor: u32, split: u32) !Verdict {
    var m = meter.Meter{};
    try meter.feed(&m, clip.samples()[start .. start + count]);
    if (meter.rms(&m) < floor) return .silent;
    const c = try zero_cross.count_crossings(clip, start, count, t_low, t_high);
    if (c >= split) return .unvoiced;
    return .voiced;
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    const band_low: i32 = -1000;
    const band_high: i32 = 1000;

    // 1. LOUD AND PERIODIC -> VOICED -- a strong signal that stays high for a long run then low for a long run crosses FEW times (well below a high voice_split)
    //    yet carries large RMS. Classify voiced.
    {
        // eight big-positive then eight big-negative: rms large, exactly one crossing across the whole span
        const sig = [_]i16{ 20000, 20000, 20000, 20000, 20000, 20000, 20000, 20000, -20000, -20000, -20000, -20000, -20000, -20000, -20000, -20000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        const v = try classify(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, 500, 6);
        if (v != .voiced) return error.LoudPeriodicNotVoiced;
    }

    // 2. LOUD AND NOISY -> UNVOICED -- a strong signal that alternates every sample crosses MANY times (at or above a small voice_split) with large RMS. Classify
    //    unvoiced.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000, 20000, -20000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        const v = try classify(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, 500, 6);
        if (v != .unvoiced) return error.LoudNoisyNotUnvoiced;
    }

    // 3. QUIET -> SILENT -- a low-amplitude signal whose RMS falls below the floor classifies silent whatever its crossing count. Use a small alternating signal
    //    (many crossings) with RMS well under the floor.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 50, -50, 50, -50, 50, -50, 50, -50 }; // rms ≈ 50, many crossings
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        const v = try classify(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, 500, 2);
        if (v != .silent) return error.QuietNotSilent;
    }

    // 4. THE FLOOR DOMINATES THE CROSSING RATE -- the SAME quiet, many-crossing signal that would be "unvoiced" by crossing rate alone is SILENT because its RMS
    //    is below the floor: the energy gate is read first. (Set voice_split low so crossings alone would say unvoiced.)
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 40, -40, 40, -40, 40, -40 }; // many crossings, rms ≈ 40
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        // by crossing rate alone (split 1) this is unvoiced; the floor (500) makes it silent instead.
        const v = try classify(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, 500, 1);
        if (v != .silent) return error.FloorDidNotDominate;
    }

    // 5. THE CRUX -- over several spans and settings, classify equals a hand-built decision from the SAME two readers (count_crossings and a Meter's RMS). The
    //    classifier is the fusion rule, not a third reader that could disagree.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 15000, -15000, 300, -300, 8000, 8000, -8000, -8000, 60, -60, 25000, -25000, 25000, -25000 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        const floors = [_]u32{ 0, 100, 1000, 5000 };
        const splits = [_]u32{ 1, 3, 7 };
        var fi: u32 = 0;
        while (fi < floors.len) : (fi += 1) {
            var si: u32 = 0;
            while (si < splits.len) : (si += 1) {
                const got = try classify(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                const want = try by_hand(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, floors[fi], splits[si]);
                if (got != want) return error.NotTheTwoReadersFused;
            }
        }
    }

    // 6. READ-ONLY -- the source clip is byte-for-byte unchanged after classifying (both features read, none write).
    {
        const src = [_]i16{ 111, 20000, -20000, 222, 50, -50, 333 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &src);
        _ = try classify(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, 500, 3);
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &src)) return error.ClassifyingWrote;
    }

    // 7. THE FLOOR-ZERO CONVENTION -- with silence_floor 0, an all-zero span (rms 0, zero crossings) classifies VOICED (the low-crossing branch), NOT silent
    //    (rms < 0 is never true). Deliberate degenerate.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        const v = try classify(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, 0, 3);
        if (v != .voiced) return error.FloorZeroNotVoiced;
    }

    // 8a. AN ILLEGAL FLOOR -- above the peak ceiling -- refuses BadFloor, the clip untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (classify(&clip, 0, clip.len, band_low, band_high, meter.sample_peak_max + 1, 3)) |_| return error.BadFloorSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadFloor) return error.WrongBadFloor;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedMutatedFloor;
    }

    // 8b. AN INVERTED BAND refuses BadThreshold (through ALES92's precheck).
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 });
        if (classify(&clip, 0, 3, 1000, -1000, 500, 3)) |_| return error.InvertedSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadThreshold) return error.WrongInverted;
    }

    // 8c. AN OUT-OF-RANGE SPAN refuses BadRange before any read, the clip untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        const three = [_]i16{ 1, 2, 3 };
        try load_clip(&clip, &three);
        if (classify(&clip, 2, 5, band_low, band_high, 500, 3)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &three)) return error.RefusedMutatedRange;
    }

    // 9. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE -- classifying a sub-span reads only [start, count). Put loud noisy samples OUTSIDE the span and a quiet run INSIDE it; the verdict
    //    must be silent (from the inner samples alone), proving the outer loud samples were not read into the features.
    {
        const sig = [_]i16{ 30000, -30000, 40, -40, 40, -40, 30000, -30000 }; // indices 2..5 are the quiet inner span
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &sig);
        const v = try classify(&clip, 2, 4, band_low, band_high, 500, 1);
        if (v != .silent) return error.SpanLeakedIntoFeatures; // if the loud outer samples had been read, rms would clear the floor
    }

    print("GREEN ales-voiced: the VOICED/UNVOICED/SILENT classifier — the first Lotus rung to FUSE two analysis readers into one verdict, the oldest decision " ++
        "in speech processing. ENERGY (ALES13's RMS) separates sound from silence; CROSSING RATE (ALES93's count, read through the ALES92 band) separates the " ++
        "two kinds of sound above the floor — voiced (periodic, few crossings) from unvoiced (noise, many crossings). The rule, energy gate FIRST: rms < " ++
        "silence_floor → silent; else crossings ≥ voice_split → unvoiced; else → voiced. The crux: it invents no new measurement — its verdict equals a " ++
        "hand-built decision from count_crossings and a Meter's RMS over the SAME span, so it can never drift from the two readers it composes. Loud-and-" ++
        "periodic reads voiced; loud-and-noisy reads unvoiced; quiet reads silent whatever the crossing rate; the floor dominates (a quiet many-crossing span " ++
        "is silent, not unvoiced); the verdict is exactly the two readers fused across many floors and splits; the source clip is byte-for-byte unchanged " ++
        "(read-only); the floor-zero convention holds (floor 0 disables silence, an all-zero span reads voiced); an illegal floor refuses BadFloor, an " ++
        "inverted band refuses BadThreshold, an out-of-range span refuses BadRange, the clip untouched; and the span discipline holds (only [start, count) is " ++
        "read into the verdict). Purely local, read-only — a three-valued label over one span, NOT a phoneme or a transcript; no adaptive floor, no time " ++
        "windowing, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
